  "abstract": "Widrow and Hoff stored neural-network weights in memistors, three-terminal electrochemical cells, in 1960; lithium-ion, metal-oxide and protonic ECRAM are their modern descendants. We ask, in simulation, how close such devices come to digital training when every weight update is made of device pulses and the reads are kept ideal (fp32). We compare plain SGD and three Tiki-Taka-family algorithms on five device specifications, on MNIST, a CIFAR-10 subset and a byte-level language model, with five seeds that share initialisation and data order across arms. Against SGD with exact updates at the same learning rate, 4 pairings (Li-ECRAM and protonic-inspired, each with chopped TTv2 or AGAD) stay within the pre-set margins on all three tasks, while plain SGD exceeds them on every published device. Against a separately tuned digital Adam no pairing does: on the language model the best analog result is 23.36% worse in bits per byte. Over a factor of four in width no arm's gap grows detectably beyond the margin; endpoint equivalence was established for one CIFAR pairing only, and an analysis paired by seed detects sub-margin growth for another. A sensitivity sheet with three seeds establishes only a few one-at-a-time ranges and shows that an earlier asymmetry limit was mostly lost weight range. Against explicit pulse streams the aggregate update holds on 10 of 12 probes and preserves the ranking of the tested cells (one failing SGD cell moves by more than the pre-set 1 pp), but fails where clipping removes nearly all of an update. A conditional energy projection, borrowing an analog inference chip's read efficiency, gives 17.9-32.4x less energy per weight-row than an H100, for cells that pass the margin under ideal reads. This version repairs the simulator after two independent reviews and reports how much each repair moved the original results.\n",
